        <h1>Nepal's Electric Revolution Is Racing Ahead</h1>

        <p class="subtitle">
            How clean energy, government incentives and Chinese
            automakers are transforming transportation in Nepal.
        </p>

        <div id="byline">
            By Lydia DePillis and Bhadra Sharma
            <br>
            Reporting from Kathmandu, Nepal
        </div>

        <p class="date">July 28, 2025 · Updated July 29, 2025</p>

        <figure>
            <img src="images/nepal-factory.jpg"
                 alt="Industrial buildings and a vehicle facility surrounded by Nepal's green mountains">
            <figcaption>
                Vehicle facilities sit in a valley surrounded by
                Nepal's mountainous landscape.
                <span>Photo: The New York Times</span>
            </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>
            Kathmandu's crowded streets have long been filled with
            buses, motorbikes, trucks and taxis. For millions of
            residents, traveling through the city means dealing with
            congestion, noise and air pollution. Now, electric vehicles
            are beginning to change the way people move around Nepal.
        </p>

        <p>
            Over the past year, electric vehicles accounted for
            76 percent of passenger vehicles sold in the country.
            Just five years earlier, their share of the market was
            practically nonexistent. The rapid change has made Nepal
            an important example of how developing countries can
            adopt cleaner transportation.
        </p>

        <blockquote>
            “For us, using electric vehicles is a comparative advantage.”
            <cite>— Mahesh Bhattarai, Nepal's Department of Customs</cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>
            Nepal's abundant hydropower has helped make the transition
            possible. Investments in electricity infrastructure have
            created a foundation for charging vehicles, while government
            tax policies have made electric cars less expensive than
            comparable gasoline-powered models.
        </p>

        <figure>
            <img src="images/kathmandu-traffic.jpg"
                 alt="Cars, buses and motorbikes filling a busy street in Kathmandu">
            <figcaption>
                Congested streets illustrate the transportation
                challenges facing Kathmandu.
                <span>Photo: The New York Times</span>
            </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2>Powering a New Market</h2>

        <p>
            China has also played a major role. As the world's leading
            manufacturer of electric vehicles, Chinese companies have
            brought a growing selection of models into Nepal. Competition
            among automakers has given consumers more choices and
            helped make electric vehicles increasingly attractive.
        </p>

        <p>
            The government and private businesses have expanded charging
            infrastructure across the country. Hotels, restaurants and
            other businesses have installed chargers, creating new
            services for drivers traveling between cities.
        </p>

        <figure>
            <img src="images/ev-charging.jpg"
                 alt="Electric vehicle charging station in Nepal">
            <figcaption>
                Charging infrastructure will be essential to supporting
                Nepal's growing electric vehicle market.
                <span>Photo: The New York Times</span>
            </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2>Challenges Ahead</h2>

        <p>
            The transition is not without challenges. Charging stations
            are not available everywhere, and drivers have concerns
            about battery replacement and repairs. Changes to government
            taxes or subsidies could also slow the adoption of electric
            vehicles.
        </p>

        <p>
            Passenger cars are only part of the problem. Many residents
            depend on motorbikes and buses, which continue to contribute
            to urban pollution. Expanding electric public transportation
            could help Nepal make its streets cleaner and more accessible.
        </p>

        <p>
            Nepal's experience shows how clean electricity, public policy
            and international manufacturing can accelerate a major
            technological shift. Whether that progress continues will
            depend on long-term investment and consistent government
            support.
        </p>

